    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-m/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Each of the terms of an arithmetic series is added to the corresponding terms of a geometric series, forming a new series with first term 3 8 and second term 13 16 . The common difference of the arithmetic series is four times as large as the first term of the geometric series. The common ratio of the geometric series is twice as large as the first term of the arithmetic series. Determine the possible values of the first term of the geometric series. ( ) 8\" style=\"--madas-width:590.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Write a for the first arithmetic term and b for the first geometric term. [B1] [A1] Let d be the common difference and r the common ratio. The first two arithmetic terms are a, a + d and the first two geometric terms b, br. The new terms are a + b, a + d + br. Using r = 2a, d = 4b, we get a + d + br = a + 4b + 2ab. Then a + b = 3/8 [A1] and (a + 4b) + (2a)b = 13/16. [A1] Eliminate a = 3/8 −b, expand and multiply by 16: 3 3  = 13 8 −b + 4b + 2b 8 −b 16 3 8 + 3b + 3 4b −2b2 = 13 16 6 + 48b + 12b −32b2 = 13 Rearrangement gives 32b2 −60b + 7 = (8b −1)(4b −7) [M1] [A1] = 0.\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The possible terms are b = 1 8 or 7 4 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 8 marks.</p>"
