    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) For 0 &lt; θ &lt; π/2, the tangent gradient is (4 cos θ)/(−2 sin θ) = −2 cot θ. [M1] Use the point-slope equation at (2 cos θ, 4 sin θ):\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q9-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"y −4 sin θ = −2 cot θ(x −2 cos θ). Therefore sin θ(y −4 sin θ) = −2 cos θ(x −2 cos θ). [M1] [A1] Expand the two sides and collect the intercept terms: y sin θ −4 sin2 θ = (−2 cos θ)x + 4 cos2 θ y sin θ + 2x cos θ = 4 cos2 θ + 4 sin2 θ y sin θ + 2x cos θ = 4(cos2 θ + sin2 θ) y sin θ + 2x cos θ = 4 This uses sin2 θ + cos2 θ = 1. [M1] The formula also gives the vertical and horizontal tangents at the respective endpoints: [A1] (b) For a triangle with two finite intercepts, 0 &lt; θ &lt; π/2. [M1] Its intercepts are 2/ cos θ and 4/ sin θ, from y = 0 and x = 0, respectively:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q9-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Its area is 1 2(2/ cos θ)(4/ sin θ) = 8/(2 sin θ cos θ), or 8/ sin 2θ. [A1] This is least when sin 2θ = 1, giving minimum area 8. In the permitted interval 2θ = π/2, so t = π/4 [A1] and √ √ P = ( 2, 2 2) . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 9 is 9 marks.</p>"
