    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q10-question-1.svg\" alt=\"During a chemical reaction a compound is formed, whose mass y grams in time t minutes satisfies the differential equation dy dt = ( − )( − ) t ≥ k 1 2 y 1 3 y , 0 , where k is a positive constant. a) Solve the differential equation to show that 1 2 − y ln 1 3 = kt + C , − y\" style=\"--madas-width:568.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q10-question-2.svg\" alt=\"where C is a constant. ( ) 7 When the chemical reaction started there was no compound present, and when t = ln 4 the mass of the compound has risen to 0.25 grams. b) Show further that 1 − t 1 e − 2 = ( ) 5 y . 1 − t 3 − 2e 2 c) State, with justification, the limiting value of y as t gets large. ( ) 1\" style=\"--madas-width:593.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q10-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) By partial fractions: On the branch with 0 ≤y &lt; 1/3, write dy = k(1 −2y)(1 −3y) dt, so dy/[(1 −2y)(1 −3y)] = k dt. To find the partial fractions, clear the denominator and substitute the two roots: 1 A B (1 −2y)(1 −3y) ≡ 1 −2y + 1 −3y 1 ≡A(1 −3y) + B(1 −2y) y = 1 2 1 = −1 2A A = −2 y = 1 3 1 = 1 3B\" style=\"--madas-width:552.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q10-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"B = 3 This gives 1 2 3 (1 −2y)(1 −3y) = − 1 −2y + 1 −3y. [M1] Separate the variables [M1] set up the two partial fractions [B1] determine the first coefficient [B1] determine the second coefficient The two antiderivatives are ln |1 −2y| −ln |1 −3y|, while the right side gives kt + C. Combining the logarithms gives ln((1 −2y)/(1 −3y)) = kt + C . [M1] [M1] [A1] (b) At t = 0, y = 0, we have C = 0. [B1] [A1] At t = ln 4, y = 1/4, the ratio is 2, so k = ln 2/ ln 4 . [A1] = 1/2\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q10-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Now e−t/2(1 −2y) = 1 −3y, [A1] giving the following explicit expression: Before substituting k = 1/2, rearrange 1 −2y 1 −3y = ekt 1 −2y = (1 −3y)ekt 1 −2y = ekt −3yekt 3yekt −2y = ekt −1 y(3ekt −2) = ekt −1 y = ekt −1 3ekt −2 Substitute k = 1/2 and multiply numerator and denominator by e−t/2 to obtain y = 1 −e−t/2 3 −2e−t/2 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-m/q10-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"(c) As t →∞, e−t/2 →0 and y →1/3 g . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
